<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Thinking on the Fly: Test-Time Reasoning
<br class="ltx_break">Enhancement via Latent Thought
<br class="ltx_break">Policy Optimization</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Wengao Ye
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Oxford
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">wengao.ye@kellogg.ox.ac.uk</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yan Liang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University College London
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">zczqlib@ucl.ac.uk</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Lianlei Shan
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of the Chinese Academy of Sciences
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">shanlianlei18@mails.ucas.edu.cn</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) have shifted from explicit Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning to more efficient latent reasoning, where intermediate thoughts are represented as vectors rather than text. However, latent reasoning can be brittle on challenging, out-of-distribution tasks where robust reasoning is most critical. To overcome these limitations, we introduce Latent Thought Policy Optimization (LTPO), a parameter-free framework that enhances LLM reasoning entirely at test time, without requiring model parameter updates. LTPO treats intermediate latent “thought” vectors as dynamic parameters that are actively optimized for each problem instance. It employs an online policy gradient method guided by an intrinsic, confidence-based reward signal computed directly from the frozen LLM’s own output distributions, eliminating the need for external supervision or expensive text generation during optimization. Extensive experiments on five reasoning benchmarks show that LTPO not only matches or surpasses strong baselines on standard tasks but also demonstrates remarkable robustness where others fail. Most notably, on highly challenging AIME benchmarks where existing latent reasoning baselines collapse to near-zero accuracy, LTPO delivers substantial improvements, showcasing a unique capability for complex reasoning. Our code is available at <a href="https://github.com/ltpo2025/LTPO" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/ltpo2025/LTPO</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) have dramatically advanced the state of reasoning in artificial intelligence, propelled by <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Chain-of-Thought (CoT)</span> prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, which enhances performance by decomposing complex problems into explicit intermediate reasoning steps in natural language. Despite its effectiveness, the explicit generation of CoT is often inefficient and costly, both in computational resources and inference latency. To overcome this, recent research has focused on <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">latent reasoning</em>, which encodes the intermediate “thoughts” as continuous hidden vectors within the model’s latent space. Notable approaches such as Coconut <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and SoftCoT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> have demonstrated that by replacing textual CoT with learned latent embeddings, models can attain similar reasoning accuracy with increased computational efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, a major limitation of existing latent reasoning approaches is their brittleness when handling challenging, out-of-distribution tasks. Methods relying on offline trained projection or latent modules typically fail to generalize to hard problems or new domains in practice, as shown in our experiments, advanced latent reasoning baselines can completely collapse on competition-grade benchmarks where robust reasoning is most critical.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we propose <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Latent Thought Policy Optimization (LTPO)</span>, a parameter-free and entirely test-time framework for boosting LLM reasoning capabilities. Unlike prior latent reasoning approaches that fix the intermediate representations, LTPO treats the <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">latent thought vectors</em> as dynamic parameters to be actively optimized at test time. Concretely, given a prompt augmented with <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> special <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">latent thought tokens</em>, we iteratively refine their hidden vectors in a closed-loop process: at each step, the current latent states are perturbed, passed through the frozen LLM (no model weight updates), and evaluated by an <em id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">intrinsic confidence-based reward</em> which is computed directly from the model’s output distributions, without textual decoding. This reward signal guides a policy gradient update, improving the latent thoughts to maximize model’s predictive certainty. After a small number of optimization steps, the refined latent thought vectors are concatenated with the prompt’s embeddings, and passed through the LLM to generate the final answer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">LTPO is simple, general, and requires no additional data or model parameter updates, enabling <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">on-the-fly</em> enhancement of LLM reasoning abilities, purely via test-time latent space optimization. Extensive experiments on five mathematical reasoning benchmarks demonstrate that LTPO not only matches or surpasses strong baselines on standard tasks, but remains robust where others fail. Most notably, on highly challenging AIME competition benchmarks where existing latent reasoning methods collapse to near-zero accuracy, LTPO delivers substantial improvements: for instance, with Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, LTPO achieves 16.67% and 13.33% accuracy on AIME2024 and AIME2025 respectively, dramatically outperforming all competitive baselines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our key contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose LTPO, a test-time framework for LLM reasoning, which directly optimizes intermediate latent thought vectors via policy gradient, without any model fine-tuning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce an efficient online Reinforcement Learning (RL) loop using a self-derived confidence-based reward, computed from the LLM’s own output distributions, eliminating the need for extra supervision or expensive text generation during optimization.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We empirically show that LTPO delivers robust reasoning performance on diverse and challenging benchmarks, addressing critical failure points of previous latent reasoning approaches.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The pursuit of advanced reasoning in LLMs has evolved from generating explicit textual rationales, known as CoT prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kojima et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, to more efficient latent reasoning approaches. Latent reasoning represents intermediate “thoughts” as continuous vectors in the model’s hidden space rather than as discrete text, aiming to match the performance of explicit CoT with lower latency. Most methods in this area rely on an offline training phase to learn these latent representations. For example, iCoT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2024</a>)</cite> fine-tunes a LLM on problems and their detailed reasoning steps to generate both the reasoning and final answer in a single forward pass; Coconut <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> directly trains the model to use the last hidden state of the “thought token” as the next input embedding; Similarly, SoftCoT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> utilizes a lightweight assistant model to generate soft thought tokens that are then projected into the LLM’s latent space using a trained projection module. Although improving efficiency, these approaches can be brittle, especially on challenging, out-of-distribution problems where adaptability is most needed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A parallel line of research has applied RL to enhance LLMs. The dominant paradigm, originating with Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, involves fine-tuning the model’s parameters using algorithms like PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite> to align with human preferences. This approach has been extended by more efficient methods like DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and specialized techniques for reasoning like GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and DAPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, these methods universally operate by updating the model’s weights in an offline training stage, based on external reward signals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our method, LTPO, diverges from both of these established research directions. It operates at the intersection of latent reasoning and optimization but introduces a new paradigm: enhancing reasoning entirely at test time without updating any model parameters. This approach is situated within the growing interest in test-time optimization strategies, which have recently been explored for instance-level adaption at test time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, LTPO operationalizes this concept through a distinct mechanism: it establishes a self-contained RL loop where the reward signal is derived directly from the LLM’s own output distributions. This bypasses the need for explicit text generation or external evaluation within the RL loop, allowing for a more effective and efficient optimization of the latent reasoning path.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04182v1/LTPO.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/440;" width="548" height="440" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of LTPO. The framework iteratively refines the embedding vectors of the <em id="S2.F1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">latent thought tokens</em> via a test-time RL loop. A confidence-based reward, calculated from the LLM’s output logits, guides the Test-time RL Module to update the latent thought vectors. After optimization, the refined vectors are concatenated with the prompt’s embeddings and passed through the LLM to generate the final answer.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LTPO</span>, a parameter-free framework that enhances the reasoning abilities of LLMs at test time. Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Thinking on the Fly: Test-Time ReasoningEnhancement via Latent ThoughtPolicy Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the overview of our framework.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Latent Thought Representation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{{\bm{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{{\bm{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a LLM with frozen parameters <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a standard input prompt which is a sequence of discrete tokens <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}=(x_{1},...,x_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}=(x_{1},...,x_{N})</annotation></semantics></math>, the model’s embedding layer <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> maps it to a sequence of vectors <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E({\bm{x}})=({\bm{e}}_{1},...,{\bm{e}}_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E({\bm{x}})=({\bm{e}}_{1},...,{\bm{e}}_{N})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{e}}_{i}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{e}}_{i}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the model’s hidden dimension.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To enable latent reasoning, we augment the input prompt with <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> special placeholder tokens, which we call <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">latent thought tokens</em>. Their initial embedding vectors, denoted by <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}^{(0)}=({\bm{h}}_{1}^{(0)},...,{\bm{h}}_{K}^{(0)})\in{\mathbb{R}}^{K\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}^{(0)}=({\bm{h}}_{1}^{(0)},...,{\bm{h}}_{K}^{(0)})\in{\mathbb{R}}^{K\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, are obtained by passing the placeholder tokens (e.g., <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[THINK]</span>) through the model’s embedding layer:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle({\bm{h}}_{1}^{(0)},...,{\bm{h}}_{K}^{(0)})=E(\mathrm{[THINK]}_{1},...,\mathrm{[THINK]}_{K})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>THINK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>THINK</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle({\bm{h}}_{1}^{(0)},...,{\bm{h}}_{K}^{(0)})=E(\mathrm{[THINK]}_{1},...,\mathrm{[THINK]}_{K}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These latent thought vectors <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> serve as the parameters that will be optimized at test time. The full input to the LLM at any point is the concatenation of the original prompt embeddings and the current latent thought vectors, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E({\bm{x}})\oplus{\bm{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>𝑯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E({\bm{x}})\oplus{\bm{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Optimization via Test-Time Reinforcement Learning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formulate the problem of finding the optimal latent thought vectors <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a sequential decision-making task, which we solve using a direct policy gradient method over <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> timesteps.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Policy as latent space exploration.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The optimization process iteratively refines the latent thought vectors <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which themselves serve as the parameters of our policy. We define the components of this process as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">State:</span> The state at timestep <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is the current set of latent thought vectors, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}^{(t)}=({\bm{h}}_{1}^{(t)},...,{\bm{h}}_{K}^{(t)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}^{(t)}=({\bm{h}}_{1}^{(t)},...,{\bm{h}}_{K}^{(t)})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Action:</span> An action <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a candidate for the next state of the latent thoughts. The action space is continuous, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}^{(t)}=({\bm{a}}_{1}^{(t)},...,{\bm{a}}_{K}^{(t)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}^{(t)}=({\bm{a}}_{1}^{(t)},...,{\bm{a}}_{K}^{(t)})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{a}}_{k}^{(t)}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{a}}_{k}^{(t)}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Policy:</span> We employ a simple yet effective stochastic policy that explores the latent space by sampling actions from a multivariate Gaussian distribution centered at the current state <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{A}}^{(t)}\sim\pi(\cdot|{\bm{H}}^{(t)})={\mathcal{N}}({\bm{H}}^{(t)},{\bm{\sigma}}^{2}{\bm{I}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>𝑯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝑯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝝈</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{A}}^{(t)}\sim\pi(\cdot|{\bm{H}}^{(t)})={\mathcal{N}}({\bm{H}}^{(t)},{\bm{\sigma}}^{2}{\bm{I}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\sigma}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝝈</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\sigma}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a variance hyperparameter that controls exploration and is decayed over time. Sampling an action is equivalent to applying a random perturbation, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}^{(t)}={\bm{H}}^{(t)}+{\bm{\epsilon}}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}^{(t)}={\bm{H}}^{(t)}+{\bm{\epsilon}}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\epsilon}}^{(t)}\sim{\mathcal{N}}({\bm{0}},{\bm{\sigma}}^{2}{\bm{I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝝈</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\epsilon}}^{(t)}\sim{\mathcal{N}}({\bm{0}},{\bm{\sigma}}^{2}{\bm{I}})</annotation></semantics></math>. This encourages local exploration around promising areas of the latent thought space.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Confidence-based reward.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To guide the optimization without external supervision, we introduce an intrinsic reward signal derived directly from the LLM’s own output distributions. Unlike recent works that leverage confidence for post-hoc selection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, our application is distinct. LTPO employs confidence as a dense reward signal to actively guide an online optimization process within the latent space for each problem instance. The intuition is that a well-formed reasoning path should lead the model to be more confident in its subsequent predictions. While this intrinsic signal is powerful, we acknowledge that model confidence is not a perfect proxy for correctness, a potential limitation we discuss in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Limitation: The Divergence of Confidence and Correctness ‣ Thinking on the Fly: Test-Time ReasoningEnhancement via Latent ThoughtPolicy Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a candidate action <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> (a probe of next state), we compute the reward by first passing it through the frozen LLM <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{{\bm{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{{\bm{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> along with the prompt embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain output probability distributions over the vocabulary <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> for each individual token:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(P_{1}^{(t)},...,P_{|{\bm{x}}|+K}^{(t)})=\mathrm{softmax}({\mathcal{M}}_{{\bm{\theta}}}(E({\bm{x}})\oplus{\bm{A}}^{(t)}))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(P_{1}^{(t)},...,P_{|{\bm{x}}|+K}^{(t)})=\mathrm{softmax}({\mathcal{M}}_{{\bm{\theta}}}(E({\bm{x}})\oplus{\bm{A}}^{(t)})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We define the confidence for a single latent thought vector <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{a}}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{a}}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> as the mean negative log-probability of the top-<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> most probable tokens predicted from that position:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle C({\bm{a}}_{i}^{(t)})=-\frac{1}{k}\sum_{v\in\mathrm{top}(k,P_{i}^{(t)})}\log P_{i}^{(t)}(v)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>top</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>log</mi><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C({\bm{a}}_{i}^{(t)})=-\frac{1}{k}\sum_{v\in\mathrm{top}(k,P_{i}^{(t)})}\log P_{i}^{(t)}(v),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{top}(k,P_{i}^{(t)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>top</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{top}(k,P_{i}^{(t)})</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> most probable tokens in the distribution <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>. A higher confidence score indicates the model is more certain about its prediction. The final reward for the entire sequence of latent thoughts is the average confidence across all <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> vectors:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R({\bm{A}}^{(t)})=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{i=1}^{K}C({\bm{a}}_{i}^{(t)})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R({\bm{A}}^{(t)})=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{i=1}^{K}C({\bm{a}}_{i}^{(t)}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Latent thought update with policy gradient.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since the reward function <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is non-differentiable with respect to the input latent vectors, we cannot use standard backpropagation. Instead, we can use a direct policy gradient method, such as REINFORCE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Williams, 1992</a>)</cite>, to update the policy parameters (i.e., the latent vectors). The objective is to find latent thought vectors <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> that maximize the expected reward:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle J({\bm{H}})=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{A}}\sim\pi(\cdot|{\bm{H}})}[R({\bm{A}})]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝑯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑨</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J({\bm{H}})=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{A}}\sim\pi(\cdot|{\bm{H}})}[R({\bm{A}})].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The gradient of this objective is given by the policy gradient theorem:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{{\bm{H}}}J({\bm{H}})=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{A}}\sim\pi(\cdot|{\bm{H}})}[R({\bm{A}})\nabla_{{\bm{H}}}\log\pi({\bm{A}}|{\bm{H}})]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝑯</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝑯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑨</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝑯</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑨</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝑯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{{\bm{H}}}J({\bm{H}})=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{A}}\sim\pi(\cdot|{\bm{H}})}[R({\bm{A}})\nabla_{{\bm{H}}}\log\pi({\bm{A}}|{\bm{H}})].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For our Gaussian policy (Equation <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Policy as latent space exploration. ‣ 3.2 Optimization via Test-Time Reinforcement Learning ‣ 3 Method ‣ Thinking on the Fly: Test-Time ReasoningEnhancement via Latent ThoughtPolicy Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), the log-probability gradient term can be simplified to:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{{\bm{H}}}\log\pi({\bm{A}}|{\bm{H}})=\frac{({\bm{H}}+{\bm{\epsilon}})-{\bm{H}}}{{\bm{\sigma}}^{2}}=\frac{{\bm{\epsilon}}}{{\bm{\sigma}}^{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝑯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑨</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝑯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>+</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝑯</mi></mrow><msup><mi>𝝈</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><msup><mi>𝝈</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{{\bm{H}}}\log\pi({\bm{A}}|{\bm{H}})=\frac{({\bm{H}}+{\bm{\epsilon}})-{\bm{H}}}{{\bm{\sigma}}^{2}}=\frac{{\bm{\epsilon}}}{{\bm{\sigma}}^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This yields a Monte Carlo estimate of the gradient from a single sample <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\epsilon}}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\epsilon}}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> at each optimization step <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{{\bm{H}}}J({\bm{H}}^{(t)})\approx R({\bm{H}}^{(t)}+{\bm{\epsilon}}^{(t)})\frac{{\bm{\epsilon}}^{(t)}}{{\bm{\sigma}}^{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝑯</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝑯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mi>𝝈</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{{\bm{H}}}J({\bm{H}}^{(t)})\approx R({\bm{H}}^{(t)}+{\bm{\epsilon}}^{(t)})\frac{{\bm{\epsilon}}^{(t)}}{{\bm{\sigma}}^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The latent thought vectors are then updated via standard gradient ascent:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{H}}^{(t+1)}={\bm{H}}^{(t)}+\eta\cdot R({\bm{H}}^{(t)}+{\bm{\epsilon}}^{(t)})\frac{{\bm{\epsilon}}^{(t)}}{{\bm{\sigma}}^{2}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝑯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mi>𝝈</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{H}}^{(t+1)}={\bm{H}}^{(t)}+\eta\cdot R({\bm{H}}^{(t)}+{\bm{\epsilon}}^{(t)})\frac{{\bm{\epsilon}}^{(t)}}{{\bm{\sigma}}^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is the learning rate. This update rule intuitively pushes the latent thoughts <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> in the direction that yields higher rewards.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">After <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> optimization steps, the set of the optimized latent thought vectors <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> is concatenated with the prompt embeddings and passed through the LLM to autoregressively generate the final answer:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{y}}=\mathrm{Decoder}({\mathcal{M}}_{{\bm{\theta}}}(E({\bm{x}})\oplus{\bm{H}}^{\ast}))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Decoder</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{y}}=\mathrm{Decoder}({\mathcal{M}}_{{\bm{\theta}}}(E({\bm{x}})\oplus{\bm{H}}^{\ast})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present our experimental setup, results, and analysis to validate the effectiveness of LTPO. Addtional implementation details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Experimental Details ‣ Thinking on the Fly: Test-Time ReasoningEnhancement via Latent ThoughtPolicy Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our method on four open-source reasoning LLMs from two distinct model families: LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct, LLaMA-3.2-3B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and Qwen-3-14B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. These models are fully open-source for reproducibility, covering multiple parameter scales to test robustness and generalizability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al. (2024)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we focus on mathematical reasoning for evaluation. We conduct experiments on five datasets: GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, MATH-500 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, ASDiv <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Miao et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, AIME2024 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mathematical Association of America, 2024</a>)</cite>, and AIME2025 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mathematical Association of America, 2025</a>)</cite>. GSM8K and MATH-500 are commonly used for evaluating the performance of reasoning systems, thus providing a solid basis for comparative analysis. ASDiv is a benchmark consisting of varied, multi-step elementary school math word problems. AIME2024 and AIME2025 are high-difficulty mathematical competition problems which are widely adopted in recent evaluations of top reasoning LLMs (e.g., Gemini-2.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google, 2025</a>)</cite>, Grok-4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">xAI, 2025</a>)</cite>, GPT-5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite>) and featured in the MathArena leaderboard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Balunović et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Specifically, we utilize the augmented version of ASDiv (ASDiv-Aug) from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2025b)</a></cite> to ensure that the models encounter novel instances, given that LLaMA and Qwen are well-trained LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare LTPO against three strong and directly comparable baselines to provide a thorough evaluation of its effectiveness: (1) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Zero-Shot CoT</span>: Our foundational baseline is the zero-shot CoT prompting method from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kojima et al. (2022)</a></cite>. This approach represents the standard for eliciting reasoning in the <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">discrete token space</em> by instructing the model to generate explicit, step-by-step thinking. On modern LLMs, this technique sets a very strong performance baseline, often exceeding fine-tuned models, making it an essential point of comparison. (2) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Zero-Shot CoT-Unk</span>: We directly append untuned <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[UNK]</span> tokens to the prompt to perform CoT reasoning. This baseline isolates the contribution of our test-time optimization procedure for latent thought tokens. (3) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SoftCoT</span>: To provide a more direct and advanced comparison for our latent reasoning framework, we adopt SoftCoT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, which performs reasoning in the <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">continuous latent space</em>. It has been shown to outperform methods like LoRA fine-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and Coconut <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> on various datasets.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All results are reported in accuracy (%). For each problem, we perform greedy decoding to generate the final answer. The generated output is then parsed to extract the final numerical answer, which is compared against the ground truth.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Performance of LTPO vs. baselines across four models and five reasoning benchmarks, reported in accuracy (%). The optimal results are in bold and the suboptimal ones are underlined.</figcaption>
