<figure id="A4.T9" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 9: </span>Prompt Example 2 for LTPO on AIME2024.</figcaption>
<div id="A4.T9.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:397.5pt;height:475.4pt;vertical-align:-235.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-7.7pt,9.2pt) scale(0.962856957305488,0.962856957305488) ;">
<table id="A4.T9.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A4.T9.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T9.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_tt">
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:397.5pt;">
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.T9.2.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T9.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T9.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:397.5pt;">
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Solve the following math problem efficiently and clearly:</span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_p">- For simple problems (2 steps or fewer):</span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_p">Provide a concise solution with minimal description.</span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_p">- For complex problems (3 steps or more):</span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_p">Use this step-by-step format:</span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.2.1.1.6" class="ltx_p">## Step 1: [Brief calculations]</span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.2.1.1.7" class="ltx_p">## Step 2: [Brief calculations]</span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.2.1.1.8" class="ltx_p">…</span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.2.1.1.9" class="ltx_p">IMPORTANT: Regardless of the approach, you MUST always put your final answer within $\boxed{{}}$.</span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.2.1.1.10" class="ltx_p">PROBLEM: There exist real numbers <math id="A4.T9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.T9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, both greater than 1, such that <math id="A4.T9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{x}\left(y^{x}\right)=\log_{y}\left(x^{4y}\right)=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{x}\left(y^{x}\right)=\log_{y}\left(x^{4y}\right)=10</annotation></semantics></math>. Find <math id="A4.T9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.2.1.1.11" class="ltx_p">There are 8 special tokens that contain compressed latent reasoning information
that might be useful for your reasoning.</span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.2.1.1.12" class="ltx_p">If these tokens are useful for your case, you can use them as reference. If these tokens are not useful
for your case, you can ignore them and focus back to solving the problem.</span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.2.1.1.13" class="ltx_p">Here are the 8 special tokens: <span id="A4.T9.2.1.2.1.1.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[UNK][UNK][UNK][UNK][UNK][UNK][UNK][UNK]</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T9.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T9.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top"></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T9.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T9.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:397.5pt;">
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.T9.2.1.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Answer:</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T9.2.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T9.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:397.5pt;">
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">## Step 1: We are given the equations <math id="A4.T9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{x}\left(y^{x}\right)=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{x}\left(y^{x}\right)=10</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.T9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{y}\left(x^{4y}\right)=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{y}\left(x^{4y}\right)=10</annotation></semantics></math>. We can rewrite these equations using the properties of logarithms: <math id="A4.T9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{x}\left(y^{x}\right)=x\log_{x}(y)=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{x}\left(y^{x}\right)=x\log_{x}(y)=10</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.T9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{y}\left(x^{4y}\right)=4y\log_{y}(x)=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{y}\left(x^{4y}\right)=4y\log_{y}(x)=10</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_p">## Step 2: Let’s denote <math id="A4.T9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{x}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{x}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A4.T9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.T9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{y}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{y}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A4.T9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we have the equations <math id="A4.T9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xa=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xa=10</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.T9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4yb=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4yb=10</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_p">## Step 3: From the first equation, we can express <math id="A4.T9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A4.T9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\frac{10}{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\frac{10}{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Substituting this into the second equation, we get <math id="A4.T9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4y\log_{y}(x)=4yb=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4y\log_{y}(x)=4yb=10</annotation></semantics></math>, which simplifies to <math id="A4.T9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=\frac{10}{4y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>10</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=\frac{10}{4y}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.5.1.1.4" class="ltx_p">## Step 4: We also know that <math id="A4.T9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=\frac{1}{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>a</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=\frac{1}{a}</annotation></semantics></math>. Substituting <math id="A4.T9.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\frac{10}{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\frac{10}{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get <math id="A4.T9.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=\frac{1}{\frac{10}{x}}=\frac{x}{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mfrac><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mn>10</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=\frac{1}{\frac{10}{x}}=\frac{x}{10}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.5.1.1.5" class="ltx_p">## Step 5: Equating the two expressions for <math id="A4.T9.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A4.T9.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{x}{10}=\frac{10}{4y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>10</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{x}{10}=\frac{10}{4y}</annotation></semantics></math>. Solving for <math id="A4.T9.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of <math id="A4.T9.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, we get <math id="A4.T9.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=\frac{100}{4y}=\frac{25}{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>100</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mi>y</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=\frac{100}{4y}=\frac{25}{y}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.5.1.1.6" class="ltx_p">## Step 6: Substituting <math id="A4.T9.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=\frac{25}{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mi>y</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=\frac{25}{y}</annotation></semantics></math> into the equation <math id="A4.T9.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xa=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xa=10</annotation></semantics></math>, we get <math id="A4.T9.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{25}{y}\cdot a=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mi>y</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{25}{y}\cdot a=10</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A4.T9.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\frac{10}{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\frac{10}{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A4.T9.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{25}{y}\cdot\frac{10}{x}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mi>y</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{25}{y}\cdot\frac{10}{x}=10</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.5.1.1.7" class="ltx_p">## Step 7: Simplifying the equation, we get <math id="A4.T9.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{250}{xy}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>250</mn><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{250}{xy}=10</annotation></semantics></math>. Solving for <math id="A4.T9.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A4.T9.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy=\frac{250}{10}=25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>250</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy=\frac{250}{10}=25</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.5.1.1.8" class="ltx_p">The final answer is: $\boxed{25}$</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T9.2.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T9.2.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top"></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T9.2.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T9.2.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.7.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:397.5pt;">
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.7.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.T9.2.1.7.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Prediction:</span></span>
<span id="A4.T9.2.1.7.1.1.2" class="ltx_p">25</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T9.2.1.8" class="ltx_tr">
